Title: Makefile resolves DATA_DIR from the environment like the Python side
Created: 2026-07-27
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-07-27T15:52Z HDMX-coding-agent created
2026-07-27T15:55Z HDMX-coding-agent note Found while wiring ticket 0354's deposit gate: make could not reach a corpus the Python scripts read fine

--- body ---
## Context

`DATA_DIR` is machine-dependent — the corpus lives on a separate disk on
padme, and a worktree has no corpus of its own — but the Makefile hardcodes it:

```make
DATA_DIR     := data/catalogs      # Makefile:37
```

Python resolves the same location from the environment
(`pipeline_loaders.py`: `os.environ.get("CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA")`, falling back
to `<repo>/data`), and `build/build_datapaper_archive.sh:27` honours the same
override:

```bash
DATA_DIR="${CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA:-$PROJ_ROOT/data}/catalogs"
```

Two of the three entry points respect the override; `make` does not. The
Makefile's own comment claims otherwise — "Python scripts resolve the same
paths via utils.py" (line 35) — which holds only while the variable is unset.

The symptom is a prerequisite `make` believes is unbuildable while the script
it would run reads the file perfectly well:

```
make: *** No rule to make target 'data/catalogs/extended_works.csv',
      needed by 'data/derived/deposit/climate_finance_corpus.csv'.
```

Found while wiring ticket 0354's deposit gate from a worktree. The workaround
was symlinking 35 corpus files into the worktree — throwaway scaffolding for
what should be a one-line fix.

## Relevant files

- `Makefile:37` — the hardcoded `DATA_DIR`; line 35's comment overstates the
  agreement with Python.
- `scripts/pipeline_loaders.py:56-58` — the `CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA` override
  Python honours.
- `build/build_datapaper_archive.sh:27` — the shell precedent for the same
  override.
- `.claude/rules/architecture.md` § Data location — documents the override as
  the way to relocate data, so the Makefile is the exception to its own rule.

## Actions

1. Resolve `DATA_DIR` from the environment with the repo-local default:
   `DATA_DIR ?= $(if $(CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA),$(CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA),data)/catalogs`.
2. Correct the line-35 comment to say all three entry points — make, Python,
   and the archive scripts — read one override.
3. Check the other data-root-derived Make variables (`DERIVED` in `paths.mk`,
   and any reference to `pool/`, `exports/`, `syllabi/`) for the same
   hardcoding and fix them in the same commit — one logical unit, per the
   rename-sweep rule.

## Test

An adherence test asserting the Makefile does not hardcode the data root: grep
the Makefile and `paths.mk` for an assignment of a data path that does not
reference `CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA`, and fail on a match. Source inspection, no
subprocess — the shape `tests/test_phase_layout.py` already uses to parse the
Makefile for path discipline.

Red first: the test fails on today's `DATA_DIR := data/catalogs`.

## Verification

- [ ] `CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA=/abs/path make check-corpus` succeeds with no
      corpus in the worktree and no symlinks
- [ ] Unset, `make` resolves `data/catalogs` exactly as now
- [ ] `make deposit-validate` runs in a fresh worktree given only the override

## Invariants

- The no-override default is unchanged, so nothing moves on a machine that
  keeps data in-repo.
- `tests/test_phase_layout.py` keeps passing: it reads Make variables to check
  that no Phase-2 output lands under `data/catalogs/`, so a variable expanded
  through an environment lookup must still parse for it.
- No script gains a second way to find the corpus — this removes a divergence
  rather than adding a mechanism.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `make` honours `CLIMATE_FINANCE_DATA` for every data-root path
- [ ] Adherence test fails on a hardcoded data root (red→green)
- [ ] Makefile comment describes the actual behaviour
- [ ] `make check` passes
